<p>
In 1850, George Gabriel Stokes -- an Irish mathematician and physicist -- set out to study the asymptotics of the <em>Airy function</em>. In doing so, he observed an incredibly strange behavior, now called the <em>Stokes phenomenon</em>.
</p>

<p>
The goal of this article is to explain what Stokes discovered. To do that, we first need to explain what the Airy function is, what it means to study its asymptotics, and why Stokes was doing this. 
</p>

<h2>The Airy function</h2>

<p>
In a lot of these articles, to give an interesting explanation of some mathematical phenomenon, we have to invent some pseudo-history of how the phenomenon <em>could</em> have been discovered. But with the Stokes phenomenon, we don't need to guess: in his paper <a href="https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/gold/pdfs/teaching/Stokes1850.pdf">On the Numerical Calculation of a Class of Definite Integrals and Infinite Series</a>, Stokes tells us!
</p>

<p>
The story begins with George Biddell Airy, an English mathematician and astronomer. Airy was trying to study the mathematics of rainbows. In this article, we won't go into the physics of rainbows -- instead focusing more on Stokes' work -- but we will briefly say what Airy was up to. 
</p>

<p>
You've surely seen a rainbow before. However, you might not have seen a <em>supernumerary rainbow</em>. A supernumerary rainbow is a rare natural phenomena, where after the usual red-orange-yellow-green-blue-indigo-violet bands of a rainbow, additional, fainter bands appear. TODO PICTURE
</p>

<p>
Descartes' theory of optics was able to explain ordinary rainbows, but not supernumerary rainbows. Airy was attempting to make a theory of optics that would explain these extra bands. TODO explain
</p>

<p>
So, the name of the game in understanding rainbows is to understand the integral
\[\int_0^{\infty} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}(w^3 - mw)\right) \, dw,\]
where \(m\) is a parameter; the values of \(m\) for which this integral is zero are the locations at which new bands will appear. 
</p>

<p>
At this point, Airy faced a big problem: how does one actually calculate this integral? It's really hard -- especially in the era before computers! 
</p>

