<h2>Approximations</h2>

<p>
We're trying to approximate
\[\operatorname{Ai}(x) = \frac{1}{\pi}\int_0^{\infty}\cos\left(\frac{t^3}{3}+xt\right) \, dt.\]
How?
</p>

<p>
The most naive way to approximate a function is to take its <em>Taylor expansion</em>. To compute the Taylor expansion, one needs to know all the higher derivatives of \(\operatorname{Ai}(x).\) Fortunately, those aren't so hard to calculate, since we can interchange differentiation and integration to get
\[\operatorname{Ai}'(x) = \frac{1}{\pi}\int_0^{\infty} \frac{d}{dx}\cos\left(\frac{t^3}{3} + xt\right) \, dt,\]
which the chain rule simplifies to 
\[\operatorname{Ai}'(x) = \frac{1}{\pi}\int_0^{\infty} -t\sin\left(\frac{t^3}{3} + xt\right) \, dt.\]
</p>

<p>
If we differentiate again, we get
\[\operatorname{Ai}''(x) = \frac{1}{\pi}\int_0^{\infty} -t^2\cos\left(\frac{t^3}{3} + xt\right) \, dt.\]
At this point, it might seem we have to go on differentiating forever, and that each derivative we get will be an even stranger looking integral. However, this new integral can actually be simplified, using integration by parts: TODO
