<p>
The first observation is that the Airy function is the solution to a differential equation. I want to emphasize something rather unusual here: usually in physics, one describes the natural world in terms of differential equations, and then one has to <em>solve</em> the differential equation to get the answer. But Airy actually derived his integral from a direct calculation, without doing any differential equations. So, in some sense we're doing the reverse of how physics usually goes: nature has given us a function, and we're trying to find a differential equation it solves! 
</p>

<p>
What does this equation buy us? 
</p>

<p>
The key observation is that it is a specific type of differential equation, called a <em>linear ODE</em>, with polynomial coefficients. We won't develop the full theory of differential equations here, but we'll mention that linear ODEs with polynomial coefficients are quite well understod, and have been understood for a very long time. So, Stokes knew that any function \(f(x)\) satisfying
\[f''(x) - xf(x) = 0\]
can be written in the form
\[A \cdot x^{-1/4}e^{2x^{3/2}/3} + B \cdot x^{-1/4}e^{-2x^{3/2}/3},\]
for constants \(A\) and \(B.\) 
</p>

<p>
Airy computed values of the Airy function from \(z=-4\) to \(z=+4\) by evaluating the integral numerically. 
</p>

